        "exam_type": "WAEC",
        "subject": "Electronics",
        "topic": "Common-Emitter Amplifier",
        "question_type": "Objective",
        "exam_year": "2021",
        "question_text": "<p>The voltage gain of a common-emitter amplifier is given by $A_v = -g_m R_C$. What does $g_m$ represent?</p>",
        "question_image": null,
        "answer_image": null,
        "explanation": "<p>In the voltage gain formula for a common-emitter amplifier, $A_v = -g_m R_C$, the term $g_m$ represents the <b>transconductance</b> of the transistor. It is defined as the ratio of the change in collector current to the change in base-emitter voltage ($g_m = \frac{\\Delta I_C}{\\Delta V_{BE}}$).</p>",
        "reference_passage": null,
        "novel": null,
        "comments_count": 0,
        "options": [
            {
                "option_id": "73708",
                "tag": "a",
                "text": "Input resistance",
                "is_correct": false
            },
            {
                "option_id": "19155",
                "tag": "b",
                "text": "Transconductance",
                "is_correct": true
            },
            {
                "option_id": "56606",
                "tag": "c",
                "text": "Output resistance",
                "is_correct": false
            },
            {
                "option_id": "41635",
